# Copy this into a repo as .env.claudebox (preferred — the launcher auto-selects it
# over .env, keeping claudebox creds separate from the project's own .env) or as .env,
# and fill in. Pass to the container with `docker run --env-file <file>`.
# NOTE: keep your real env file out of git (already covered by .dockerignore/.gitignore).
#
# DON'T QUOTE VALUES. `docker run --env-file` is not a shell: it keeps everything
# after the '=' literally, quotes included, so ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL="https://..."
# becomes a value that really does start and end with a quote. Write
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://... instead — spaces and colons in a value are fine
# unquoted. (The entrypoint strips a matched surrounding pair and warns, but
# don't rely on that.)

# --- Model provider ---------------------------------------------------------
# Which backend Claude Code talks to: ollama (default) | anthropic | custom |
# cloudflare | workersai. Fill in ONLY the credential block for the provider you
# choose below.
# PROVIDER=ollama

# ollama (default): Ollama Cloud's native Anthropic-compatible API.
# API key — https://ollama.com/settings/keys
OLLAMA_API_KEY=

# anthropic: Anthropic's own API. Set PROVIDER=anthropic and provide ONE of the
# credentials below (REVIEW_MODEL defaults to claude-opus-4-8):
#   - ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from https://console.anthropic.com/ , OR
#   - CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN to reuse your `claude` subscription login: run
#     `claude setup-token` on your host and paste the token here (works on any
#     host OS), OR
#   - mount your host ~/.claude read-write (Linux host only) so the reviewer
#     finds ~/.claude/.credentials.json — no variable needed. See README.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# custom: any other Anthropic-compatible endpoint. Set PROVIDER=custom, a
# BASE url only -- Claude Code appends /v1/messages itself, so a base URL that
# already ends in an endpoint path 404s every request (a startup error here).
# REVIEW_MODEL the endpoint serves, the base URL, and ONE auth credential:
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN (Bearer header) or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (x-api-key header).
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=

# cloudflare: a Cloudflare AI Gateway. Set PROVIDER=cloudflare, a REVIEW_MODEL
# the upstream serves, and GATEWAY_UPSTREAM to say which upstream the gateway
# fronts: anthropic (the default) | bedrock | vertex. Model IDs differ per upstream (Anthropic
# claude-opus-4-8, Bedrock us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-5-v1:0, Vertex
# claude-opus-4-5@20251101), so REVIEW_MODEL is always required here.
# ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS carries the gateway token; on bedrock/vertex it's the
# ONLY credential (this container holds no AWS/GCP credentials and Claude Code's
# own cloud auth is skipped), so it's required for those two. Don't set the
# CLAUDE_CODE_USE_*/CLAUDE_CODE_SKIP_*_AUTH switches — GATEWAY_UPSTREAM picks the
# upstream and the entrypoint sets them; a contradicting value is a startup error.
# PROVIDER=cloudflare
# GATEWAY_UPSTREAM=bedrock
# ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS=cf-aig-authorization: Bearer <CF_AIG_TOKEN>
#
#   SEVERAL headers: Claude Code wants one per line, which this file can't hold
#   (one KEY=VALUE per line, no continuation, no escapes). Separate them with a
#   literal \n, or use one numbered variable each (up to _20) — both spellings
#   are assembled into the multi-line value inside the container, and mixing
#   them is fine (numbered ones are appended in index order):
# ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS=cf-aig-gateway-id: my-gw\ncf-aig-authorization: Bearer <CF_AIG_TOKEN>
# ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS_1=cf-aig-gateway-id: my-gw
# ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS_2=cf-aig-authorization: Bearer <CF_AIG_TOKEN>
#
#   GATEWAY_UPSTREAM=anthropic — base URL, plus ANTHROPIC_API_KEY holding an
#   ANTHROPIC API key. That's the UPSTREAM credential and is separate from the
#   gateway token in ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS above; Cloudflare's docs show the
#   gateway token reused here, which only works if the gateway has a stored
#   provider key to inject. Don't use ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN for a console key —
#   Anthropic accepts Bearer only for OAuth tokens (`claude setup-token`), so a
#   key there fails every request with "x-api-key header is required":
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<ACCOUNT_ID>/<GATEWAY_ID>/anthropic
#
#   GATEWAY_UPSTREAM=bedrock — base URL only (the header is the credential):
# ANTHROPIC_BEDROCK_BASE_URL=https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<ACCOUNT_ID>/<GATEWAY_ID>/aws-bedrock/bedrock-runtime/<AWS_REGION>/
#
#   GATEWAY_UPSTREAM=vertex — base URL, GCP project, and region:
# ANTHROPIC_VERTEX_BASE_URL=https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<ACCOUNT_ID>/<GATEWAY_ID>/google-vertex-ai/v1
# ANTHROPIC_VERTEX_PROJECT_ID=<GCP_PROJECT_ID>
# CLOUD_ML_REGION=us-east5

# workersai: a model from Cloudflare's Workers AI catalog (glm-5.2, Kimi, ...),
# reached through a LiteLLM translator that runs inside the container on loopback.
# Workers AI models are served ONLY over an OpenAI-compatible schema and Claude
# Code speaks only the Anthropic one, so the translator is what bridges them --
# unlike PROVIDER=cloudflare, which needs no translator but cannot serve @cf/
# models at all. Two settings, because the endpoint is derived from the account:
#   CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID  the account the models are billed to
#   CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN   an API token with the Workers AI Read permission
#                          (dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens) -- a token,
#                          NOT the Global API Key
# The token stays behind the translator and is never handed to Claude Code.
# REVIEW_MODEL defaults to @cf/zai-org/glm-5.2 (the same model the default ollama
# provider uses). Other choices: @cf/moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code,
# @cf/moonshotai/kimi-k2.6, @cf/zai-org/glm-4.7-flash. Browse the catalog at
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/models/ and pick one that lists
# function calling -- a reviewer without tool use cannot read the diff.
# PROVIDER=workersai
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
# Ports the two in-container processes listen on (loopback only): the translator,
# and the normalizer behind it. Change them only if something else in the
# container already uses 4000/4001; they must not be the same port.
# LITELLM_PORT=4000
# SHIM_PORT=4001
# The normalizer sits between the translator and Cloudflare and fixes exactly one
# thing: LiteLLM leaves out the `content` field on an assistant message that
# carries only a tool call. That is valid OpenAI and glm-5.2 accepts it, but the
# Kimi models reject it ("Invalid value at messages[N].content"), and Claude Code
# emits such a message on every tool call -- so for those models nearly every
# review pass would fail. Setting this to 0 removes the hop; there is no reason to
# unless it is causing a problem, since filling in an empty string is valid for
# every model.
# SHIM_NORMALIZE=1
# Log the translated request/response bodies to litellm.log -- the only practical
# way to see what the translator actually sent when a model rejects a request.
# Those bodies include your token, so leave this off for unattended runs.
# LITELLM_DEBUG=0

# --- GitHub -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Privilege-minimized GitHub token. Needs only: read repo contents, read PRs &
# comments, write PR comments (issues:write / pull_requests:write). MUST NOT
# have push/merge/admin scopes.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Target repository, "owner/repo".
GITHUB_REPOSITORY=

# --- PR selection (choose EXACTLY ONE) --------------------------------------
# Which PRs to review. Set exactly one of the following (or pass the matching
# launcher flag). Zero or more-than-one is a startup error.
#   PR_ALL=1                     review all open PRs                (--all)
#   PR_ASSIGNEE=alice            open PRs assigned to a GitHub user (--assignee)
#   PR_IDS=12,15,20              exactly these PR numbers           (--prs)
#   PR_SEARCH=is:open label:x    PRs matching a gh search query     (--search)
# PR_ALL=
# PR_ASSIGNEE=
# PR_IDS=
# PR_SEARCH=

# --- Optional ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Model to use. Must be one your chosen PROVIDER actually serves. Leave unset to
# take the provider default (ollama: glm-5.2:cloud, anthropic: claude-opus-4-8,
# workersai: @cf/zai-org/glm-5.2); REQUIRED for PROVIDER=custom and
# PROVIDER=cloudflare. For Ollama, browse
# https://ollama.com/search?c=cloud
# — the :cloud suffix is stable but exact version strings change, so verify yours.
# REVIEW_MODEL=glm-5.2:cloud

# Seconds to sleep between review passes.
REVIEW_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# Rotate a PR's Claude session to a fresh one after this many successful passes
# on that PR, to cap the context growth of its long-lived resumed session.
# Applies per PR. 0 = never rotate.
MAX_PASSES_PER_SESSION=0

# Optional Linear access. Set this and the reviewer can read the Linear ticket a
# PR references (description AND comments) and flag where the change diverges
# from what the ticket asked for. Linear's MCP server takes an API key directly,
# so no interactive login is needed: Settings -> Security & access -> Personal
# API keys (https://linear.app/settings/account/security).
# USE A READ-ONLY KEY. Give it the Read permission only. The reviewer runs with
# all permission prompts skipped, so a write-capable key would let it change your
# tickets — same reasoning as the read-mostly GITHUB_TOKEN.
# Leave unset to disable Linear entirely.
# LINEAR_API_KEY=

# HOST_REPO_PATH is set automatically by the launcher's --export-sessions flag
# (it's the host path the working clone runs at, so exported session folders
# line up with your host ones). You normally don't set this yourself; it's here
# only so a hand-rolled `docker run` can opt into the same alignment.
# HOST_REPO_PATH=

# Where your primary repo is mounted (read-only) inside the container. The
# reviewer makes a cheap local clone of this; it must be the primary repo, not a
# git worktree of it.
# REPO_PATH=/repo

# Override the review instruction used when a PR's session STARTS (defaults to a
# thorough per-PR review prompt; see entrypoint.sh). Use the {{PR}} token where
# the PR number should appear — the harness substitutes it per PR.
# REVIEW_PROMPT=

# Override the instruction used when a PR's session is RESUMED on a later cycle
# (it already holds that PR's prior context, so this just nudges a re-check).
# Also supports the {{PR}} token.
# FOLLOWUP_PROMPT=

# Append extra instructions to REVIEW_PROMPT / FOLLOWUP_PROMPT, on top of
# whichever is in effect — the built-in default (Linear stanza included, if
# LINEAR_API_KEY is set) or your override above. Also supports the {{PR}}
# token; a single space is inserted between the prompt and the suffix.
# REVIEW_PROMPT_SUFFIX=
# FOLLOWUP_PROMPT_SUFFIX=

# The entrypoint verifies the container was started with the security hardening
# flags (see README) and refuses to run if a security boundary is missing. Set
# to 1 to downgrade those hard failures to warnings — only for a non-Docker
# runtime or a deliberate test, never for the normal unattended deployment.
# ALLOW_UNHARDENED=0
